{"name":"Green Lane","hex":"#cad6c4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Green Lane","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A borough in Montgomery County, Pennsylvania, United States.","A place in the United Kingdom:\nA hamlet in Ilsington parish, Teignbridge district, Devon, England . \nA hamlet in Yarkhill parish, Herefordshire, England .\nA hamlet in Studley parish, Stratford-on-Avon district, Warwickshire and Redditch borough, Worcestershire, England, on a road of the same name .\nAn outer suburb of Coventry, West Midlands, England, with a road of the same name .\nA hamlet in Abermule with Llandyssil community, Powys, Wales ."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Green_Lane"}],"entries":[{"title":"Green lane (road)","blocks":[{"text":"A green lane is a type of road in the United Kingdom, usually an unmetalled or unpaved rural route."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Grendon%2C_Northamptonshire_byway.jpg/330px-Grendon%2C_Northamptonshire_byway.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green_lane_(road)","wikidata":"Q5603539"},{"title":"Green Lane (Stockport)","blocks":[{"text":"Green Lane was a football ground in the Heaton Norris suburb of Stockport, England. It was the home ground of Stockport County between 1889 and 1902, and was used during the club's first two seasons in the Football League."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green_Lane_(Stockport)","wikidata":"Q16632811"},{"title":"Lane","blocks":[{"text":"In road transport, a lane is part of a roadway that is designated to be used by a single line of vehicles to control and guide drivers and reduce traffic conflicts. Most public roads (highways) have at least two lanes, one for traffic in each direction, generally separated by lane markings. On multilane roadways and busier two-lane roads, lanes are generally designated with road surface markings. Major highways often have two multi-lane roadways separated by a median."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Montague_Expressway.jpg/330px-Montague_Expressway.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lane","wikidata":"Q3222002"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Green_Lane_near_Longley_Green_-_geograph.org.uk_-_813083.jpg/330px-Green_Lane_near_Longley_Green_-_geograph.org.uk_-_813083.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Green_Lane_near_Longley_Green_-_geograph.org.uk_-_813083.jpg/330px-Green_Lane_near_Longley_Green_-_geograph.org.uk_-_813083.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An overgrown green lane in Worcestershire.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGreen_Lane_near_Longley_Green_-_geograph.org.uk_-_813083.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Start_of_Green_Lane_sign_in_Jersey.jpg/500px-Start_of_Green_Lane_sign_in_Jersey.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Start_of_Green_Lane_sign_in_Jersey.jpg/1280px-Start_of_Green_Lane_sign_in_Jersey.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Start_of_Green_Lane_sign_in_Jersey.jpg/500px-Start_of_Green_Lane_sign_in_Jersey.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Green Lane signage in Jersey","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStart_of_Green_Lane_sign_in_Jersey.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Ambulance_lane.jpg/500px-Ambulance_lane.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Ambulance_lane.jpg/1280px-Ambulance_lane.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Ambulance_lane.jpg/500px-Ambulance_lane.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An ambulance lane in Warsaw, Poland","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAmbulance_lane.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/The_Basketweave.jpg/500px-The_Basketweave.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/The_Basketweave.jpg/1280px-The_Basketweave.jpg?utm_source=e